cartridge "THE FARM GAME/Daniel M. Seurer/1985"

' THE FARM GAME
' A ColecoVision game based on "The Farm Game" by Daniel M. Seurer,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette, October 1985, p. 44 (Commodore 64 and VIC-20 16K,
' BASIC; orig/farmgame64.bas). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you run a 200-acre farm (five fields of 40 acres) for 1 to 25
' years, starting with $20,000 in the bank. Each year prices inflate, you
' may read the market news and your finances and sell stored grain on the
' futures market, then you plant wheat, corn or soybeans in each field and
' choose how much insecticide and herbicide to spread. The weather and
' the pests decide the yield; the market decides the price. At harvest
' you sell what you want (the rest goes to storage), pay at least a third
' of your loan, and pay the bills. A negative balance must be covered by
' a loan at 12% (credit line $50,000); past the line you are bankrupt.
' At the end stored grain is sold, the loan repaid: $20,000 or more wins.
'
' From the original: every price, rate and formula (README.md lists
' them): seed prices and seed per acre, base crop prices, yields per acre,
' fuel per acre, $70 an acre, $2,000 overhead, $4,000 harvesting, $1 a
' unit chemicals, inflation (none, or 1-5%, applied twice a year as in
' lines 50/2110-2180), the weather (two draws, line 2200), pest years
' halving the chemicals' effect, the effect curves sqrt(units)/5 and
' sqrt(units)/5 + .12 capped at 1.1, field qualities 1.1/1.03/1/.98/.9,
' the four market factors per crop and the futures price rule (2360-2610),
' storage at $.015 a bushel a month, the loan rules (minimum a third,
' 12% on borrowing and on what is left, $50,000 line), the $20,000 test.
'
' Liberties:
' - Joystick menus replace the F keys and typed numbers: amounts are set
'   digit by digit (stick), the right button jumps to the maximum (then
'   the minimum), the left button accepts. Years 1-25 (any number in the
'   64); units 0-99 a acre; borrowing and loan payments in whole dollars;
'   the smallest loan is the shortfall rounded up to the dollar.
' - Crop rotation (added): a field planted with the same crop as last year
'   yields 15% less; wheat or corn after soybeans yields 10% more. Without
'   it, corn every year with the same chemicals was the best play in
'   every year (a one-choice strategy). Shown on the planting screen.
' - Fair hints: the planting screen shows each field's quality and last
'   crop, and the chemical screens show the protection the units give
'   (the article told players 15-35 units was best).
' - Two listing slips fixed: selling the same grain twice on the futures
'   market replaced the first sale (TS(J)=K) and the sold bushels were
'   taken out of storage twice (lines 2050 and 230).
' - Money is kept in cents (i32), prices in 1/10000 dollar; the listing's
'   floating point is followed with exact integer steps, so a cent can
'   differ now and then. Stored grain is capped at 250,000 bushels a crop
'   and money at $9,999,999.99.
' - Pictures: a farm scene with the fields, a growing season with weather
'   and a tractor; the year's weather is described (the 64 printed only
'   "YOUR CROPS ARE GROWING"). No play-again question: the title follows.
'
' Amy notes: see README.md (bugs found while making it).
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick up/down chooses, left/right changes,
' a button accepts. Keypad not used.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait, display off; music first in
' the sound table, the title tune played once; `set sprite count` given
' after every `clear sprites`; nothing flashes.

